{"conjecture": "Long-running systems accumulate implicit invariants—unstated relationships between components that are never formally encoded but become load-bearing. Each locally correct change optimizes for explicit constraints while unknowingly violating these tacit dependencies, so the system's true constraint surface grows faster than its documented one. The result is that the cost of change tracks the number of implicit invariants a modification might disturb, not the complexity of the change itself."}
